WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.880
Preparation determines how much can be reconstructed during a cloud incident.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:09.300
If logs were not enabled, exported, retained, or protected before the event,

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.000
responders may be left with partial evidence.

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:17.340
A cloud incident response plan should define logging strategy, evidence retention,

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:22.840
asset inventory, identity inventory, roles, escalation paths, runbooks,

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:26.420
and access methods before the first serious alert arrives.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.120
Cloud asset inventory is foundational.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:34.220
Responders need to know which accounts, subscriptions, projects, tenants, regions,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.000
services, workloads, storage locations, and data owners exist.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.860
Identity inventory is just as important.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:48.000
User accounts, service accounts, roles, groups, federated identities, API keys,

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:49.740
automation identities,

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.800
and break-glass accounts all shape both investigation and containment.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:58.340
Logging should be centralized where practical and protected from casual modification.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:05.040
Audit logs, API activity logs, identity logs, network telemetry, storage access logs,

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:06.540
workload logs,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.840
and configuration history can all contribute to scoping and timeline reconstruction.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:16.100
Cloud security posture tools and detection rules can help identify risky configuration and

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:17.840
suspicious activity,

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.660
but they are most useful when integrated with response workflows.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.120
Preparation also includes access.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:28.580
Responders need appropriate, scoped access for investigation and containment.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.760
Break-glass access should be controlled, monitored,

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:36.780
and documented so it is available during emergencies without becoming a standing weakness.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.920
Runbooks should explain how to collect common evidence, who to notify,

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.660
and how to avoid actions that erase important context.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.200
Prepared teams can move faster because they do not spend the first hours discovering where

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:50.620
logs live,

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.880
who owns the service, or whether evidence exists.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.260
The goal is not to predict every incident.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.040
The goal is to make the environment observable and the response process repeatable enough

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.020
that uncertainty can be managed.
